,"occupations":["politician"],"wiki":"Sēmisi Sika"},"Q48778102":{"id":"Q48778102","name":"Pōhiva Tuʻiʻonetoa","desc":"Prime Minister of Tonga from 2019 to 2021","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Pohiva_Tu%CA%BBi%CA%BBonetoa_(cropped).jpg/330px-Pohiva_Tu%CA%BBi%CA%BBonetoa_(cropped).jpg","gender":"M","born":"1951-06-30","died":"2023-03-18","birthplace":"Talafoʻou","parties":["Tonga People's Party"],"education":["Monash University","Institute of Certified Management Accountants","University of the South Pacific"],"occupations":["politician","certified public accountant"],"wiki":"Pōhiva Tuʻiʻonetoa"},"Q19631293":{"id":"Q19631293","name":"Hu'akavameiliku","desc":"Prime Minister of Tonga from 2021 to 2024","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Siaosi_Sovaleni_June_2022.jpg/330px-Siaosi_Sovaleni_June_2022.jpg","gender":"M","born":"1970-02-28","died":null,"birthplace":"Tongatapu","parties":["independent politician"],"education":["University of Oxford","University of the South Pacific","Timaru Boys' High School"],"occupations":["politician"],"wiki":"Siaosi Sovaleni"},"Q8083950":{"id":"Q8083950","name":"ʻAisake Eke","desc":"Prime Minister of Tonga in 2025","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Aisake_Eke_NZHC_(cropped).jpg/330px-Aisake_Eke_NZHC_(cropped).jpg","gender":"M","born":"1960-12-26","died":null,"birthplace":null,"parties":[],"education":[],"occupations":["politician"],"wiki":"ʻAisake Eke"},"Q5437258":{"id":"Q5437258","name":"Fatafehi Fakafānua, 8th Lord Fakafānua","desc":"Prime Minister of Tonga since 2025","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Fatafehi_Fakaf%C4%81nua_January_2026.jpg/330px-Fatafehi_Fakaf%C4%81nua_January_2026.jpg","gender":"M","born":"1985-03-20","died":null,"birthplace":null,"parties":["independent politician"],"education":["University of the South Pacific"],"occupations":["politician"],"wiki":"Fatafehi Fakafānua"}},"summary":"Tonga, officially the Kingdom of Tonga, is an island country in Polynesia, part of Oceania. The country has 171 islands, of which 45 are inhabited. Its total surface area is about 750 km2 (290 sq mi), scattered over 700,000 km2 (270,000 sq mi) in the southern Pacific Ocean. As of September 2026, Tonga has a population of 103,291, 70% of whom live on the main island, Tongatapu. The country stretches approximately 800 km (500 mi; 430 nmi) north-south. It is surrounded by Fiji and Wallis and Futuna (France) to the northwest, Samoa to the northeast, New Caledonia (France) and Vanuatu to the west, Niue (the nearest foreign territory) to the east and Kermadec (New Zealand) to the southwest. Tonga is about 1,800 km (1,100 mi; 970 nmi) from New Zealand's North Island.\nTonga was first inhabited roughly 2,500 years ago by people who were a part of the Lapita culture, Polynesian settlers who gradually evolved a distinct and strong ethnic identity, language, and culture as the Tongan people. They quickly established a powerful footing across the South Pacific, and this period of Tongan expansionism and colonization is known as the Tuʻi Tonga Empire. From the rule of the first Tongan king, ʻAhoʻeitu, Tonga grew into a regional power. It was a thalassocracy that conquered and controlled unprecedented swathes of the Pacific, from parts of the Solomon Islands and the whole of New Caledonia and Fiji in the west to Samoa and Niue and even as far as parts of modern-day French Polynesia in the east. Tuʻi Tonga became renowned for its economic, ethnic, and cultural influence over the Pacific, which remained strong even after the Samoan revolution of the thirteenth century and Europeans' discovery of the islands in 1616.\nFrom 1900 to 1970, Tonga had British protected-state status. The United Kingdom looked after Tonga's foreign affairs under a Treaty of Friendship, but Tonga never relinquished its sovereignty to any foreign power. In 2010, Tonga took a decisive step away from its traditional absolute monarchy and became a semi-constitutional monarchy, after legislative reforms paved the way for its first partial representative elections.\nTonga is a member of the Commonwealth of Nations, the United Nations, the Pacific Islands Forum, and the Alliance of Small Island States.","history":"The history of Tonga is recorded since the ninth century BC. Tongatapu was the first island to be settled in Polynesia, when people from the Lapita culture settled there around 3000 years ago. The region is now controlled by the Kingdom of Tonga. Along with Samoa, Tonga served as a gateway into the rest of Polynesia.  Ancient Tongan mythologies recorded by early European explorers report the islands of 'Ata and Tongatapu as the first islands having been hauled to the surface from the deep ocean by Maui.","historyTitle":"History of Tonga","curated":null,"indicators":{"population":[[1960,66170],[1961,68037],[1962,69940],[1963,71885],[1964,73879],[1965,75924],[1966,78002],[1967,79814],[1968,81353],[1969,82885],[1970,84401],[1971,85896],[1972,87356],[1973,88805],[1974,90683],[1975,93017],[1976,95325],[1977,96725],[1978,97204],[1979,97544],[1980,97769],[1981,97909],[1982,97980],[1983,97998],[1984,97990],[1985,97984],[1986,98010],[1987,98376],[1988,98969],[1989,99403],[1990,99700],[1991,99895],[1992,100022],[1993,100103],[1994,100166],[1995,100225],[1996,100275],[1997,100674],[1998,101388],[1999,102046],[2000,102677],[2001,103288],[2002,103874],[2003,104464],[2004,105071],[2005,105671],[2006,106253],[2007,106726],[2008,107020],[2009,107203],[2010,107400],[2011,107570],[2012,107460],[2013,107078],[2014,106609],[2015,106095],[2016,105524],[2017,105356],[2018,105567],[2019,105669],[2020,105704],[2021,105490],[2022,105042],[2023,104597],[2024,104175],[2025,103742]],"gdp":[[1975,32506700],[1976,30036400],[1977,34139400],[1978,41567500],[1979,44667000],[1980,53260100],[1981,62242000],[1982,62068200],[1983,60864000],[1984,64248400],[1985,60058700],[1986,68195900],[1987,81667100],[1988,10